<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saffarid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saffarids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Saffarid dynasty was a Persian dynasty founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in the ninth century by Yaqub ibn al-Layth, a former coppersmith. It conquered much of eastern Iran and challenged the authority of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, even marching towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. Accounts of the Abbasid Caliphate list the Saffarids among the regional powers that emerged as caliphal military control weakened.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Saffarid dynasty" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HistoryofIran · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Saffarid_dynasty_(861%E2%80%931002).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Saffarid dynasty was a culturally Persianate dynasty of eastern Iranian origin that ruled parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and eastern Makran from 861 to 1002, and was one of the first indigenous Persian dynasties to emerge after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Islamic conquest</a>. Its founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-ibn-al-layth-al-saffar">Ya'qub ibn al-Layth al-Saffar</a>, was born in 840 at Karnin, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaranj">Zaranj</a> and west of Bost, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. A native of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a> and a local ayyar, he worked as a coppersmith before becoming a warlord. He moved to Zaranj, left his trade and gained the power to act as an independent ruler.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From his capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaranj">Zaranj</a>, Ya'qub moved east into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arachosia">Arachosia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindawar">Zamindawar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>; by 865 he had vanquished the Zunbils and forced the Hindu Shahis to move to Udabhanda. He then invaded Bamyan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a>, Badghis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghor">Ghor</a>, territories ruled mostly by Buddhist tribal chiefs, taking vast plunder and slaves. The Tahirid city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> fell in 870, and the Saffarids annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> in 873. They turned west to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, and in 876 came within a few days' march of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> before the Abbasid army turned them back. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> mines in the Panjshir Valley let them mint silver coins. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> recognised Ya'qub as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a>, Fars and Kerman, but he had no concept of centralised government.</p><p>Ya'qub's brother and successor Amr ibn al-Layth was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-ibn-ahmad">Ismail Samani</a> and lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, leaving the Saffarids Fars, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a>. Under Tahir ibn Muhammad (901–908) they fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> for Fars, but a civil war broke out in 908 and the governor of Fars defected to the Abbasids. In 912 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a> expelled them from Sistan, which they later regained under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-ja">Abu Ja</a>'far Ahmad ibn Muhammad, though only as a minor power. In 1002 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> invaded Sistan and dethroned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalaf-ibn-ahmad">Khalaf ibn Ahmad</a>, ending the dynasty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Saffarids patronised Persian in court poetry and gave impetus to a renaissance of New Persian literature. After Ya'qub's conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, when some poets praised the victory in Arabic, he asked his secretary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-wasif">Muhammad ibn Wasif al-Sistani</a> to compose the verses in Persian.</p><p>The religion of Ya'qub is debated, since most sources are hostile and contradict one another: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khallikan">Ibn Khallikan</a> calls him a Kharijite, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a> an Isma'ili, and the Abbasid caliph's propaganda even claimed he was a Christian. The only contemporary source, al-Ya'qubi, called him a righteous holy warrior fighting on the caliph's command. D. G. Tor argues that Ya'qub was a pious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> holy warrior in the tradition of the volunteer frontier fighters, supported by leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> scholars, an argument A. C. S. Peacock has called convincing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saffarid dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Military power passed increasingly to provincial commanders and then to outright successor dynasties: the Tahirids, <b>Saffarids</b> and Samanids in the east, and later the Buyids and Seljuks at the centre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Arab conquest, New Persian first flourished at the courts of the <b>Saffarids</b> and the Samanids, who ruled from Bukhara in the 9th and 10th centuries and encouraged poets to write in Persian, among them Rudaki, often called the father of Persian poetry.</blockquote><a href="/language/persian">Persian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Saffarids</b> took eastern Iran by force; the Samanids ruled Transoxiana and Khurasan as nominal servants of the caliph and real sovereigns of their own lands.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li><li><blockquote>The region was Islamised under the <b>Saffarids</b> and later became the heartland of the Ghaznavid Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/zunbils">Zunbils</a></li></ul></section>
